  "full_response": "No, vaccines do **not** cause autism. This claim has been thoroughly debunked by extensive scientific research and medical consensus. Here’s what the evidence shows:\n\n### **Key Points:**\n1. **Original Study Debunked** – The 1998 study by Andrew Wakefield linking the MMR vaccine to autism was **fraudulent**, retracted, and Wakefield lost his medical license. Later investigations found he manipulated data for financial gain.\n\n2. **Large-Scale Studies** – Over **100 studies** involving millions of children worldwide have found **no link** between vaccines and autism. Major health organizations, including the **WHO, CDC, NIH, and American Academy of Pediatrics**, confirm this.\n\n3. **No Biological Mechanism** – There is no credible scientific explanation for how vaccines could cause autism. Autism is a **neurodevelopmental condition** with genetic and prenatal origins, not an immune response to vaccines.\n\n4. **Thimerosal (a mercury-based preservative)** – Even though thimerosal was removed from most childhood vaccines in 2001 (except some flu vaccines), autism rates **continued to rise**, disproving any connection.\n\n5. **Vaccine Ingredients Are Safe** – Ingredients like aluminum (an adjuvant) are present in tiny, safe amounts and are eliminated by the body. They do not accumulate or cause harm.\n\n### **Why Does the Myth Persist?**\n- **Coincidental Timing** – Autism symptoms often become noticeable around the same age (12–24 months) when children receive vaccines, leading to false associations.\n- **Misinformation** – Anti-vaccine groups continue to spread fear despite overwhelming evidence.\n- **Confirmation Bias** – Parents of autistic children may seek explanations, making them vulnerable to misinformation.\n\n### **Bottom Line:**\nVaccines are **one of the safest and most effective public health tools** ever developed. They prevent deadly diseases (measles, polio, whooping cough) and **do not cause autism**. Delaying or refusing vaccines puts children and communities at risk of preventable illnesses.\n\nWould you like references to specific studies or organizations for further reading?",
